From: Marcin Krystianc <marcin.krystianc@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] common/reflink: reap _compare_range readers before returning

_compare_range() feeds cmp -s from two process substitutions running
_read_range, an xfs_io pread piped through cut. When the ranges differ,
cmp exits at the first differing byte. bash does not wait for process
substitutions, so _compare_range returns while an xfs_io can still hold
its file open.

On NFS, a caller that removes the file at that moment gets a
silly-rename instead of an unlink, and the .nfsXXXX entry makes rmdir
of the parent fail with ENOTEMPTY. generic/118 hits this in its
_cleanup, right after its last _compare_range, which is an intentional
mismatch:

    rm: cannot remove '/mnt/nfs-test-env/test/test-118': Directory not empty

The trace of a failing run shows the sequence: the unlink of file1 is
sent as a RENAME to .nfs0000000000c0020100000001, the RMDIR returns
-ENOTEMPTY, and 10 ms later the xfs_io exits and the client deletes the
.nfs file. It failed 1 in 28 iterations of generic/118 over NFSv4.2
with sec=krb5i.

Open the substitutions on named descriptors, keep their pids, and after
cmp close the read ends, so a reader blocked on a full pipe gets
SIGPIPE, then wait for both. On return no reader has a file open. The
return value is still cmp's.

---
 common/reflink | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/common/reflink
+++ b/common/reflink
@@ -200,15 +200,29 @@
 }
 
 # Compare ranges of two files
+#
+# cmp stops at the first difference, and bash does not wait for process
+# substitutions, so a reader could still hold its file open after this
+# returns.  On NFS, a caller that then removes the file gets a silly-renamed
+# .nfsXXXX entry instead, and rmdir of the parent fails with ENOTEMPTY.
+# Close the read ends so blocked readers get SIGPIPE, then reap them.
 _compare_range() {
 	file1="$1"
 	offset1="$2"
 	file2="$3"
 	offset2="$4"
 	len="$5"
+	local fd1 fd2 pid1 pid2 ret
 
-	cmp -s <(_read_range "$file1" "$offset1" "$len") \
-	       <(_read_range "$file2" "$offset2" "$len")
+	exec {fd1}< <(_read_range "$file1" "$offset1" "$len"); pid1=$!
+	exec {fd2}< <(_read_range "$file2" "$offset2" "$len"); pid2=$!
+
+	cmp -s /dev/fd/$fd1 /dev/fd/$fd2
+	ret=$?
+
+	exec {fd1}<&- {fd2}<&-
+	wait $pid1 $pid2
+	return $ret
 }
 
 # Prints the md5 checksum of a hexdump of a part of a given file
